# A HEAD-BEARING .rxt FILE. Every declaration W1 accepts, plus the three
# new block directives, plus `features only`.
#
# It exists because the corpus has NO head-bearing file — measured, 0 of
# 179 — so without it the seam (`--list-source`, the body-start skip, the
# head grammar) would ship with a population of zero and every check
# named as its detector would be green while detecting nothing. That is
# [MECH-REACH]'s failure exactly, and it is cheaper to notice here than
# two milestones later.
#
# [DD-13b.W1.2] TWO CORRECTIONS THIS FIXTURE NEEDED, AND NEITHER WAS
# VISIBLE AT W1.1, WHICH IS THE POINT. Its `lib` named a file that does
# not exist, and its `target` named the definition `greeting`, which NO
# BLOCK IN THIS FILE DECLARES. W1.1 recorded a `lib` path without opening
# it and parsed `target` without resolving it, so both were inert and
# nothing in the tree could go red. W1.2 resolves both, so the fixture had
# to become true: `common.rxt` is a real sibling fixture and the target
# names `plain_run`, a block that is here. A fixture that could not be
# built was still a perfectly good witness for the SEAM — which is what it
# was written for — and stopped being one the moment a step downstream
# started reading the declarations it carried.
description this file exercises W1's head grammar end to end
lib "common.rxt"
config dev
  pcrec --warn-emit-bytes=100000
  flags i
  features classes
  budget steps=1000000
config release from dev
  engine vm
  encoding byte
target rx = plain_run with dev, release

pattern a+
name plain_run
description a bare greedy run, the simplest thing with a head above it
m "aaa" 0 3
n "b"

pattern colou?r
name colour
encoding byte
features only classes
m "colour" 0 6
m "color" 0 5
n "colr"
